---
import Layout from "../../components/layout/BaseLayout.astro"

// English version of /accesibilidad/ (same audit, same dates). The dates come
// from the audit file (jpiedra-clientes/jpiedra/auditorias/2026-11-13/) and
// only change when the evaluation is repeated. Spanish legal names carry
// lang="es" so that a screen reader pronounces them correctly.
const evaluationDate = "13 November 2026"
const verificationDate = "16 November 2026"
const nextReview = "November 2027"
---

<Layout
    lang="en"
    title="Accessibility of jpiedra.com — WCAG 2.2 AA conformance"
    description="How accessible jpiedra.com is, in English and in Spanish: result of the evaluation against WCAG 2.2 AA and EN 301 549, scope, method and how to report a barrier."
>
    <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-6 lg:px-8 py-32 lg:py-40">
        <header class="mb-16">
            <p
                class="text-xs font-body font-medium tracking-[0.3em] text-accent uppercase mb-6"
            >
                Accessibility
            </p>
            <h1
                class="font-heading text-[clamp(2rem,4.5vw,3.5rem)] leading-[1.1] text-text-primary mb-6"
            >
                Accessibility of jpiedra.com
            </h1>
            <p class="text-lg text-text-secondary font-body leading-relaxed">
                This page tells you how accessible this website is, how that
                was checked and what to do if you find a barrier.
            </p>
            <p class="text-sm text-text-muted font-body mt-6">
                Last reviewed: {verificationDate}
            </p>
        </header>

        <div
            class="legal-content flex flex-col gap-10 text-text-primary font-body leading-relaxed"
        >
            <section
                class="border border-border-hover bg-bg-surface rounded-xl p-6 md:p-8"
            >
                <h2 class="font-heading text-2xl mb-4">Conformance status</h2>
                <p class="text-lg">
                    <strong
                        >jpiedra.com fully conforms to WCAG 2.2 level AA</strong
                    >, the version adopted by the European standard EN 301 549
                    V4.1.1, in English and in Spanish, within the scope described
                    below.
                </p>
                <p class="mt-4">
                    Of the 55 level A and AA success criteria in WCAG 2.2, 49
                    apply to this site and all 49 are met. The other 6 do not
                    apply because the site has nothing they evaluate: the only
                    video has no sound, there is no live broadcast, no transaction
                    with legal or financial effects and no sign-in. Requirement 9.7
                    of EN 301 549 V4.1.1 is met too: the site respects forced
                    colours and the text size you choose.
                </p>
                <p class="mt-4">
                    The legal reference in Spain is still, for now, UNE-EN 301 549
                    V3.2.1, which is equivalent to WCAG 2.1 AA. Against it the result
                    is the same: <strong>fully conformant</strong>, with 45
                    applicable criteria out of 50 and all 45 met.
                </p>
                <p class="mt-4 text-text-secondary">
                    “Fully conformant” means that no failure remains on the
                    pages and processes evaluated. <strong
                        >It does not mean the site is perfect</strong
                    >: in Spanish a sample was evaluated, not every page, and no
                    evaluation replaces what you find when you use it yourself.
                    If you run into a barrier, tell me: how is explained below.
                </p>
            </section>

            <section>
                <h2 class="font-heading text-2xl mb-4">Scope</h2>
                <p>
                    The evaluation covers <strong
                        >both versions of jpiedra.com</strong
                    >, English and Spanish, and the site’s complete processes.
                </p>
                <ul class="mt-4 space-y-2 list-disc pl-5">
                    <li>
                        <strong>In English:</strong> every page, with no sampling.
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <strong>In Spanish:</strong> a sample of 9 pages: the 3D home
                        page, the classic version, the web accessibility and 3D
                        experiences pages, the Law 11/2023 self-assessment tool, a
                        blog post, an accessibility report, the cookie policy and
                        the kitchen configurator case study.
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <strong>Complete processes:</strong> the contact form in both
                        languages and the self-assessment tool, including their error
                        messages.
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <strong>Also:</strong> an automated sweep of every page in
                        Spanish.
                    </li>
                </ul>
                <p class="mt-4">
                    <strong>Not covered:</strong> the specific content of the Spanish
                    pages and blog posts outside the sample, beyond the automated
                    sweep. The blog and the self-assessment tool exist only in
                    Spanish.
                </p>
            </section>

            <section>
                <h2 class="font-heading text-2xl mb-4">How it was checked</h2>
                <p>
                    <strong>Method: self-assessment.</strong> I carried out the review
                    myself. I own the site and audit web accessibility for a living,
                    and I used the same protocol I apply to any client.
                    <strong>It is not an evaluation by an independent third party</strong
                    >, and it is worth reading it with that in mind.
                </p>
                <p class="mt-4">
                    The sample was chosen with the WCAG-EM 2.0 methodology, and
                    automated testing was combined with manual checks:
                </p>
                <ul class="mt-4 space-y-2 list-disc pl-5">
                    <li>Automated testing with axe-core 4.11.4, on desktop and mobile.</li>
                    <li>
                        A keyboard walk-through, stop by stop, on every page, on
                        desktop and on mobile with the menus open, and the complete
                        processes done with the keyboard only.
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        Each of the 55 WCAG 2.2 criteria checked one by one on every
                        page, each with its procedure and its result recorded.
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        Contrast measured against the real background wherever it is
                        not a flat colour, such as the 3D relief on the home page, by
                        day, by night and with snow.
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        The <strong>NVDA</strong> screen reader on the forms, the home
                        pages and the header, in both languages, and a virtual screen
                        reader on the rest.
                    </li>
                </ul>
                <p class="mt-4">
                    <strong>Evaluation date:</strong> {evaluationDate}, on the live
                    site. It found six failures, none serious and none specific to
                    the English version: the contrast of the home page buttons when
                    pressed, the order of the mobile menu, a drawn cursor that blinked
                    endlessly, some English names not marked as English, the line above
                    the home page headline with increased text spacing, and a notice
                    that covered the last link on narrow screens. All six were fixed,
                    and on {verificationDate} they were checked again with the same test
                    that found them, together with a full pass over the sample to make
                    sure nothing else had broken.
                </p>
                <p class="mt-4">
                    Browsers and assistive technology tested: Chromium on desktop
                    (1280 px) and mobile (375 px), and NVDA on Windows. VoiceOver,
                    JAWS, TalkBack, Safari and Firefox were not tested, so nothing
                    is claimed about them.
                </p>
            </section>

            <section>
                <h2 class="font-heading text-2xl mb-4">If you find a barrier</h2>
                <p>
                    If there is anything on this site you cannot use, with a
                    keyboard, a screen reader, zoom or whatever contrast you
                    need, write to me and I will look into it:
                </p>
                <p class="mt-4 text-lg">
                    <a
                        href="mailto:contacto@jpiedra.com?subject=Accessibility%20of%20jpiedra.com"
                        class="text-accent underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-accent-hover rounded-sm focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-accent"
                        >contacto@jpiedra.com</a
                    >
                </p>
                <p class="mt-4">
                    <strong>I reply within 5 working days</strong>, telling you what
                    I checked, whether it will be fixed and when. If you need any
                    content from this site in another format, ask for it at the same
                    address and tell me which format works for you. You can write in
                    English or in Spanish.
                </p>
                <p class="mt-4 text-text-secondary">
                    As a consumer, you can also contact consumer services,
                    independently of this channel. In Spain, market surveillance
                    is the responsibility of the authorities designated by each
                    autonomous community.
                </p>
            </section>

            <section>
                <h2 class="font-heading text-2xl mb-4">
                    Why I publish this when I don’t have to
                </h2>
                <p>
                    To be clear: <strong>I am not currently required</strong> to
                    publish this. jpiedra.com is the website of a self-employed
                    professional based in Spain who designs, builds and audits
                    websites, so neither Spain’s rules for the public sector
                    (<span lang="es">Real Decreto 1112/2018</span>) nor Spain’s
                    transposition of the European Accessibility Act
                    (<span lang="es">Ley 11/2023</span>) apply to it.
                </p>
                <p class="mt-4">
                    It does fall under <strong
                        ><span lang="es">Real Decreto 193/2023</span>, article
                        14.2</strong
                    >, which requires private websites about goods and services
                    offered to the public to meet the accessibility criteria of the
                    public-sector rules (today, the standard UNE-EN 301 549). That
                    requirement applies from <strong>1 January 2029</strong> for new
                    private services and from <strong>1 January 2030</strong> for
                    existing ones. Article 14.3, which requires companies “of
                    special economic significance” to state how accessible they are,
                    does not apply either: I am well below its thresholds.
                </p>
                <p class="mt-4">
                    I evaluate against WCAG 2.2 even though the legal reference is
                    still 2.1 because the new version of the European standard, EN
                    301 549 V4.1.1, was published in September 2026 and will become
                    the reference once it is cited in the Official Journal of the
                    European Union. I would rather be early.
                </p>
                <p class="mt-4">
                    I publish this for transparency, to be ready ahead of that
                    timetable, and because someone who sells accessibility audits
                    should be able to show the state of their own website without
                    being asked.
                </p>
            </section>

            <section>
                <h2 class="font-heading text-2xl mb-4">Dates and review</h2>
                <ul class="space-y-2 list-disc pl-5">
                    <li>
                        <strong>Evaluation carried out on:</strong>
                        {evaluationDate}
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <strong>Fixes verified on:</strong>
                        {verificationDate}
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <strong>Method:</strong> self-assessment by the site’s owner
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <strong>Last review of this page:</strong>
                        {verificationDate}
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <strong>Next planned review:</strong>
                        {nextReview}, or earlier if there is a redesign or a new
                        interactive component
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </section>

            <section class="border-t border-border pt-8">
                <p class="text-sm text-text-muted">
                    This is transparency information about the accessibility of
                    the site. <strong
                        >It is not a certificate or an accreditation of legal
                        conformance</strong
                    >, and no authority has validated it. It was written by an
                    accessibility specialist, not a lawyer: how the law applies to
                    a specific case may have nuances that call for legal advice.
                </p>
            </section>
        </div>
    </article>
</Layout>
